 and mortar stores so  they saw no need to invest in a  struggling startup like netflix although  they did struggle netflix was able to  survive the bubble and the subsequent  decline in the stock market following  the september 11th attacks the company  went public on may 29 2002 selling 5.5  million shares of common stock at 15 per  share over the next several years  netflix saw tremendous growth outpacing  its brick and mortar competitors this  growth is likely due to hastings  insistence that netflix should focus on  the online portion of its model and the  company launched its online streaming  service in january of 2007. at the time  however they only had a thousand films  available for streaming compared to the  70 000 they had available for rent  by the year 2010 netflix was a  powerhouse for streaming and a dominant  player in the entertainment industry it  was this same year when blockbuster  declared bankruptcy and when netflix  acquired the rights to the television  series breaking bad many people  attribute blockbuster's demise to  netflix's sudden surge in popularity  because they're streaming rights to  shows like breaking bad but really they  fell apart because of a huge amount of  debt they had incurred and poor  management practices  throughout the 2010s netflix started to  expand its operations into other  countries and they started to gain the  rights to programming from large  companies such as warner bros the walt  disney company nickelodeon tnt and amc  when these contracts were signed netflix  realized the need to develop their own  original library before these companies  got wise and started making their own  streaming services they started  releasing original shows such as house  of cards oranges the new black bojack  horseman several joint productions with  marvel studios arrested development  narcos the crown and stranger things  stranger things could not have come at a  better time because other streaming  services were starting to gain traction  and becoming real competitors in the  streaming wars these companies such as  amazon prime video hulu hbo apple tv  plus and disney plus already had deep  libraries and stronger intellectual  properties which they could capitalize  on so as soon as their contracts expired  with netflix they could be the sole  streaming platform for their own content  the success of shows like stranger  things and house of cards were one of  the main reasons why netflix was able to  continue its growth through the mid to  late 2010s by 2020 though the company  was at a point where they seemed to be  green lighting almost any shows  resulting in a large amount of low  quality content being released most of  which went unwatched it was at this  point when the nightmare truly began  for the first time in the company's  history netflix lost subscribers in the  first quarter of 2022 they lost over 200  000 subscribers and it's expected that  they'll lose another 2 million over the  next quarter so why did this happen  where did they go wrong over the last  several months netflix has released a  slew of negative announcements including  the fact that they'd be raising their  prices while also canceling shows and  development and cutting out popular  content as their contracts expire with  other companies there have also been  rumors that the company's ceo reed  hastings is considering adding  advertisements to the platform when ads  have been nowhere to be found since the  company's beginning lastly netflix has  announced that they'll be cracking down  on password sharing which up to this  point has been more of a feature of the  platform rather than a loophole with  netflix even tweeting in 2017 love is  sharing a password  all of these events have led to a  dramatic decrease in subscribers layoffs  of almost 500 netflix employees and a  sharp fall in the company's stock price  the price per share went from 690 in  october of 2021 to just 175 dollars in  june of 2022 an almost 75 percent  decrease in the stock price in a matter  of months  if netflix hopes to survive this  downturn i believe that they need to do  three things focus on making quality  content only green light highly  marketable and merchandiseable shows and  change their release schedule let's  start with the first item though netflix  needs to focus on making quality content  alone not wasting time and resources on  all the small low quality shows that  have been greenlit over the past few  years the cost of making these shows has  gone up drastically meaning the shows  they decide to make have to be hits in  the past netflix hasn't done a very good  job of marketing their shows simply  because of a lack in the marketing  budget and because they've had too many  shows and not enough space to market  them properly on their platform if they  focus on making a smaller number of  shows it'll be easier to showcase each  of them ensuring no content is lost in  the current nightmare that is netflix  next they need to try to find new ideas  for programming that are more easily  merchandisable if you look at netflix's  most popular shows you won't find much  merchandise available online shows like  ozark the crown and bridgerton aren't  very merchandiseable and there are very  few people who would be willing to buy  merchandise for the shows even if it  were readily available for purchase  if you look at other streaming services  top shows though you'll see highly  merchandisable shows such as one  division and the mandalorian sales for  baby yoda merchandise alone have  probably been higher than the sales for  all of netflix's top shows put together  if netflix releases more highly  merchandisable shows such as their one  saving grace stranger things they'll be  able to unlock an entirely new revenue  stream and build a stronger community  around the netflix brand lastly if  netflix wants to survive they'll need to  change their release schedule currently  when netflix releases a new show they  drop all the episodes for the season on  the same day while many people enjoy  this it might actually be hurting the  company in the long run when people  binge shows they lose the sense of  wonder and enjoyment that comes when a  new episode is released each week almost  like a christmas morning effect every  week it also cuts out the room for  people to make rumors and theories about  their shows most of which ends up being  free word of mouth advertising if  netflix changed their release schedule  to once per week they wouldn't have to  make so much content either they'd be  saving money and retaining subscribers  for longer as they stay subscribed for  the duration of a show's entire release  schedule to see this in action let's  compare one of netflix's most popular  shows to one of disney pluses squid game  and obi-wan kenobi let's start with  squid game a south korean survival drama  which became netflix's most watched show  within a week of its launch every  episode was released on the same day and  the buzz surrounding the show lasted for  a couple of weeks the show provided a  pretty marketable concept but very  little room for merchandising besides  green sweatsuits and maybe masks with  the playstation buttons on them then you  look at obi-wan kenobi the star wars  mini-series following the character of  obi-wan kenobi which became disney  plus's most watched original series on  its opening weekend the series had a  weekly release schedule with two  episodes dropping on its premiere date  and one episode following each week  thereafter this left room for tons of  rumors and speculation about where the  show would be heading and the buzz  surrounding the series lasted for weeks  the character of obi-wan kenobi and his  story during this era of star wars  provides a highly marketable concept  with plenty of room for merchandising as  well  t-shirts lightsabers and tons of other  items have been sold in a frenzy over  the past several weeks far surpassing  any merchandise released for squid game  if netflix were able to take some of its  most popular tv concepts and position  them in a way that makes them more  marketable and merchandisable while also  spreading out their release schedule i  believe they'd see fantastic results  only by doing so will they be able to  survive the streaming wars and return  their share prices back to their former  status  also quit whining about password sharing  if someone's paying 15 or 20 bucks a  month they should be able to let their  roommates log in too  anyways if netflix hopes to survive this  nightmare they'll have to make some  pretty big changes but let me know what  you think should netflix start to  stretch its release schedules drop your  thoughts below also check out the video  on your screen for some different  content don't forget to like and  subscribe and thanks for watching rocket  riley  [Music]

